---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ResistorColorCode from '@/components/tools/calc2/ResistorColorCode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="resistor-color-code-calculator">
  <ResistorColorCode client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to read a resistor color code</h2>
    <p>
      Hold the resistor so that the bands are closer to the left end. Read the digit bands from the left,
      then multiply by the multiplier band. The last band gives the tolerance, and on a 6 band resistor
      the band after it gives the temperature coefficient. The resistor color code calculator does this
      for 4, 5 and 6 band resistors, and works backwards from a value to its bands.
    </p>
    <pre><code>4 bands:  digit digit multiplier tolerance
5 bands:  digit digit digit multiplier tolerance
6 bands:  digit digit digit multiplier tolerance tempco
R = digits × 10^multiplier</code></pre>

    <h2>The colour table (IEC 60062)</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Colour</th><th>Digit</th><th>Multiplier</th><th>Tolerance</th><th>Tempco</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Black</td><td>0</td><td>× 1</td><td></td><td>250 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Brown</td><td>1</td><td>× 10</td><td>±1%</td><td>100 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Red</td><td>2</td><td>× 100</td><td>±2%</td><td>50 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Orange</td><td>3</td><td>× 1k</td><td>±0.05%</td><td>15 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Yellow</td><td>4</td><td>× 10k</td><td>±0.02%</td><td>25 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Green</td><td>5</td><td>× 100k</td><td>±0.5%</td><td>20 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Blue</td><td>6</td><td>× 1M</td><td>±0.25%</td><td>10 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Violet</td><td>7</td><td>× 10M</td><td>±0.1%</td><td>5 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Grey</td><td>8</td><td>× 100M</td><td>±0.01%</td><td>1 ppm/K</td></tr>
          <tr><td>White</td><td>9</td><td>× 1G</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gold</td><td></td><td>× 0.1</td><td>±5%</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Silver</td><td></td><td>× 0.01</td><td>±10%</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>None</td><td></td><td></td><td>±20%</td><td></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Yellow, violet, red, gold: the digits are 4 and 7, red multiplies by 100, so the value is
      47 × 100 = 4,700 Ω = 4.7 kΩ. Gold is ±5%, so a good part measures between 4.465 kΩ and 4.935 kΩ.
    </p>
    <p>
      Brown, black, black, brown, brown on a 5 band resistor: 100 × 10 = 1 kΩ at ±1%.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Band colours fade with heat and age. Red and orange, and brown and red, are easy to confuse; measure with a meter when in doubt.</li>
      <li>On 4 bands a value has two significant digits. 4.75 kΩ needs a 5 band resistor.</li>
      <li>Surface-mount resistors use printed number codes, not colour bands.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
